\n| ----- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ask` | `object` | What the agent waits for in the waiting phase. Has `text`, optional `approveLabel`, `onApprove` and optional `onDecline`. |"],[233,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                                               |\n| ----- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ext` | `string` | The extension Download saves with. `csv` also shows the content as a table in the reader. |"],[302,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                                            |\n| ----- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ask` | `object` | While a step waits: the button that lets the agent go on. Has a `label` and `onAllow`. |"],[1471,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                                  |\n| ----- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `max` | `number` | How many toasts stay mounted. Older ones drop off the back. Defaults to `6`. |"],[298,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                     |\n| ----- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `url` | `string` | The address bar: the page's address, or the search being typed. |"],[1401,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                    |\n| ----- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `cwd` | `string` | The working directory, shown in the header, like `~/acme/web`. |"],[674,"| Name  | Type     | Description                                                 |\n| ----- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `min` | `string` | Minimum column width when `fit` is set. `200px` by default. |"],[1146,"| Name | Type                  | Description                                                                                                                |\n| ---- | --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ai` | `RichTextAi \\| false` | Writes the AI's suggestions. Defaults to a demo that streams canned text; pass your model call, or `false` to hide the AI. |"],[548,"| Name | Type               | Description                                    |\n| ---- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `to` | `DraftRecipient[]` | Who it goes to: `{ name; initials?; color? }`. |"],[1275,"| Name | Type            | Description                                                                    |\n| ---- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `as` | `tabs \\| radio` | `tabs` (the default) when it switches a panel, `radio` when it sets an option. |"],[1276,"| Name | Type     | Description                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id` | `string` | Ties the tabs to their panel: give the panel an id of the form \"id-panel\" and point its aria-labelledby at \"id-value\". Defaults to a generated id. |"],[1703,"| Platform                  | Tokens look like               | Where to set them                   |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |\n| **Web** (React / Angular) | `--vo-*` CSS custom properties | On `:root`, or scoped to any parent |"],[1704,"| Platform    | Tokens look like                    | Where to set them   |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| **Flutter** | `VoColors` / `VoSpace` / `VoRadius` | In the Visuno theme |"],[1723,"| URL                     | What it is                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/llms.mdx/docs/<path>` | Any single page as raw Markdown (e.g. `/llms.mdx/docs/components/status-pill`). |"],[1722,"| URL              | What it is                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/llms-full.txt` | The entire documentation inlined as one text file, ready to paste into a context window. |"],[1721,"| URL         | What it is                                                                            |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/llms.txt` | An index of the docs — every page with its title and link, for an assistant to crawl. |"],[5,"2026-11-06"],[4,"2026-11-07"],[538,"A `DonutChartSegment` is `{ label: string; value: number; color?: string }`; `color` defaults to the\nchart palette (`--vo-c1..c6`) in order."],[981,"A `ProgressMeter` is `{ label, value, max, unit?, warnAt?, dangerAt?, dangerLabel?, note? }`: it\nreads \"On track\" until `value / max` crosses `warnAt` (\"Running low\") and then `dangerAt` (the\n`dangerLabel`, or \"Almost out\")."],[1063,"A `RadarAxis` is `{ label: string; short?: string; value: number; baseline: number }`; `short` is\nshown instead of `label` on a narrow card, and `value`/`baseline` are 0 to 100."],[1185,"A `RunJob` state is queued, running, failed, or done. A `RunStep` has a label, a state, and an\noptional meta shown once the step is done. Progress defaults to the share of a job's steps that are\ndone."],[167,"A `TimelineStep` has a `label`, an optional `icon` or `brand` (an app's own mark, like `figma`,\n`github` or `slack`), a `kind` (`model`, `tool` or `app`, which picks its colour), a `start` and\n`duration` in seconds, an optional `color`, `cost`, and — for a step that failed — `failed`, a `tag`\nand a `note`, and `retry` to hang a dashed line off the step it retries. A `TimelineStat` has a\n`value`, a `label` and an optional `icon`."],[1560,"A `TraceSpan` has an `id`, a `name`, a `kind` (agent, llm, tool, or retrieval), a `start` and\n`duration` in seconds, optional `tokens`, optional `input` and `output` strings, and optional\n`children`. An agent span without its own token count adds up every span under it."],[502,"A `TunerParam` is `{ key, label, min, max, value, unit?, decimals? }`: `key` names the `--vo-<key>`\nvariable the preview reads, `unit` is appended to whole numbers (like `px`), and `decimals` shows\nand sets the value with that many decimals instead of rounding."],[1612,"A `WebPage` has a `title`, a `domain`, an optional `date`, a `status` (reading, read, skimmed,\npaywall, blocked, or timeout), and an optional `cite` number the answer refers to it by."],[267,"A badge is a compact label. Its `tone` picks the color (neutral, the accent tint, the three\nstates, or one of eight tag colors). Two sizes, an optional dot or icon before the label, and an\noptional remove button after it."],[279,"A bar chart plots a value per bar, with a readout on hover that breaks each one down by `series` and\na switch between `ranges`. The bars grow in one after another a moment after the chart appears, then\nredraw left to right on every switch while the total rolls to its new figure. Off by default each\nbar is one block in the accent; set `stacked` to split every bar into its parts in the chart palette,\nwith a legend."],[296,"A browser session shows the agent browsing the web for you: a small browser frame with the page it\nis on, the steps it takes under it, and Pause and Take over. When a step waits for you — a cookie\nbanner in the way, say — it shows why and a button to let the agent go on. Take over hands the page\nto you: its frame turns the accent colour, its controls become usable, and the button reads Hand\nback."],[314,"A button is a pill in one of the variants — `primary` for the main action, `soft` and `line` for the\nrest, `ghost` for the quietest — in three sizes. Return a promise from `onClick` and the button runs\nitself: it shows the busy face until the promise settles, then the done face. `ButtonCopy` copies\ntext and flashes a check."],[331,"A card is a surface you build other components onto. It has an optional header (a `title`, a\n`subtitle` and an end `action` slot), a body of whatever you put inside, and an optional `footer`\nof actions. `pad` sets the inset; set `interactive` (or pass `onClick`) and the whole card becomes a\nhit target that lifts on hover, takes focus, and fires on Enter or Space."],[849,"A centred dialog over a dimmed backdrop, closed by the backdrop, a button or Escape, with focus and page scroll kept in check."],[1802,"A CLI to pull components and tokens into a project."],[1400,"A compact terminal for the shell commands an agent runs: the working directory and how the run is\ngoing in the header, each command with its output streaming in under it and an exit code or a check\nwith how long it took, and a summary at the foot once it is over. Notes and edits sit between the\ncommands — a note the agent writes about what went wrong, and the edit that fixes it. Long output\nfolds to its last lines. The terminal keeps one height (420px) and its output scrolls, staying on\nthe newest line unless the reader has scrolled up."],[434,"A context meter is a pill with a ring that shows how full the context window is. It opens a panel\nwith one bar split by kind, a legend whose rows list what is in each part, and a Compact button\nthat summarises older turns to free room. The ring turns orange past a warning share, and a message\nline fades in with a warning or a result."],[467,"A design review on its way out — checks run, a teammate reviews and the agent answers, then you pick how it ships."],[680,"A design system score made of a few checks you can open."],[509,"A details panel shows a record's properties in folding sections and lets you edit them in\nplace. Each `section` holds `rows`: a `select` row picks its value from a small menu (with a\ncoloured dot, an avatar or a plain chevron), a `number` row rolls to its new value, a `tags`\nrow carries labels and an optional add button, and a plain row just shows its `value`. The\naccent flashes behind a value when it changes. A `copyValue` adds a copy button beside the\ntitle."],[343,"A diff to review hunk by hunk — keep or undo each change, or ask the agent about a line."],[530,"A donut chart shows a whole split into parts: a ring of `segments` that draw in one by one, with a\nlegend beside it. Each segment takes a `label` and a `value`, and gets a colour from the chart\npalette unless you pass one. The middle shows the total with its `label` and `unit`; hovering a\nsegment or its legend row highlights both and shows that one in the middle instead."],[249,"A drop zone, then the files as plain rows. Each file wears its type's hue — its badge, its\nprogress ring and the wash that fills its row from the left as it uploads. Once a file is up the\nagent reads it: the line shimmers, then says what it found, like `12 pages`. Pass `files` with each\none's `progress`, `reading` and `detail`; `onFiles` hands back what the person dropped or picked."],[562,"A failed step with retry, skip or hand over to a person."],[607,"A feedback sheet sits under an answer in a chat thread. The thumbs rate it; a thumbs down\nopens a sheet with quick `reasons` and a free-text box so the person can say what missed.\nSend shows a spinner, then the sheet folds away and a thank-you line lands. A thumbs up just\nnotes it. It is `bare` by default so it reads flat under a message; set it to `false` for a\ncard of its own."],[221,"A file the agent made, shown four ways with the same behaviour: a `compact` row, a `preview` card\nwith its first page, an `inline` chip inside a message, or a `stack` of its versions. Each plays a\nwriting state while `state` is `writing` — the meta line shows `status` and the actions hold back —\nthen settles when it turns `ready`. From there you can open the file in a reader, switch between its\n`versions`, Copy its content and Download it."],[217,"A file the agent made, with its versions, Open, Copy and Download."],[651,"A funnel chart shows how many people reach each `stage` in order. Each bar grows in a moment\nafter the chart appears, the stage that loses the largest share of the one before it is marked\nin red, and the agent's `insight` sits below. With more than one `view`, a segment switch redraws\nthe bars top to bottom and crossfades the note."],[671,"A grid is a layout primitive for arranging cards or any other components. Give it a fixed column\ncount with `cols`, or set `fit` to pack as many columns as fit at a minimum width (`min`) so it\nreflows on its own with no breakpoints. `gap` sets the gutter. A child spans more than one column\nwith `span` (`2`, `3`, `4`) or the full row with `span=\"full\"`. The grid is its own container, so it\ncollapses to one column when it gets narrow — whatever the page around it does."],[1586,"A hands-free voice chat on a card. The orb is a lit glass sphere that swells with whoever is\ntalking — the accent colour for you, violet for the agent — with what was said underneath and mute\nand end below. It draws what you pass through `state`, `level` and `caption`; it never opens the\nmicrophone itself. Talking while muted shows a notice, the way calls do."],[1582,"A hands-free voice chat, with an orb that shows who is talking."],[777,"A launch checklist runs the pre-flight checks before something ships. Each check is a row with a\nglyph — a waiting ring, a spinner while it runs, a pass or a fail — plus a `label` and a quiet\n`note`. A progress bar across the top carries one segment per check, tinted as each result lands.\nA failed check shows a **Fix** button; the `status` line defaults to a count, \"Checking\" while one\nruns, then what is left to fix or \"All clear, ready to ship\"."],[793,"A line chart plots a few `series` over the same `points` in time. The first series is the main one —\na solid line with a soft fill — and the rest are dashed, to compare against. The lines draw in from\nthe left while the big number counts up; move the pointer over the chart for a readout that follows\nthe cursor, and use the legend to toggle a series off (one always stays on)."],[723,"A line icon by name — or your own SVG — that takes the text colour and themes for free."],[814,"A live answer is a question and the reply that streams in beneath it, word by word. A `[1]` after a\nword turns into a numbered citation you can hover or pin, which lights up its entry in the sources\nlist. Each turn moves through `waiting`, `thinking`, `answering` and `done`; once it is `done` it\ngains the copy, rate and retry actions, the sources toggle and — on the last turn — the suggested\n`followUps`. Picking a follow-up adds a new turn below, so the thread grows instead of being replaced."],[491,"A live preview beside a few sliders, with a button that hands the tuning to the agent. Drag a\nslider — or arrow-key it — to tweak the sample card by hand, or press **Tune with agent** and it\nwalks each value to a target, turns the row it is moving accent, and drops a note on what it\nchanged. Each slider sets a `--vo-<key>` variable on the preview, so the card reshapes live."],[832,"A metric with its trend line, a one-line read on why it moved, and a breakdown of what moved it.\nA range switch (`7d` / `30d` / `90d`) re-rolls the big number and the change tag and redraws the\nline in place; the pointer reads off the nearest point; a marker sits where a release happened; and\n**Why did it move?** opens a breakdown of the drivers behind the change. Set `explaining` while you\nfetch those drivers and the button shows the agent \"Looking into it\"."],[1139,"A minimal editor for a draft an agent helps with: a formatting toolbar (heading, bold, italic,\ninline code, link, lists and quote), the text, and a quiet foot with the word count and undo. Select\ntext and press **Ask AI** — or `⌘J`/`Ctrl+J`, or type `/` on a new line — and a suggestion streams in\nbeside the text, the old words struck through and the new ones arriving word by word, to accept with\n`Tab` (or `Enter`) or discard with `Esc`. Pass your own `ai` to call a model; the default streams a\ncanned demo."],[1135,"A minimal editor with an inline AI menu that streams a suggestion to accept or discard."],[853,"A modal is a centred dialog over a dimmed backdrop. It closes on the backdrop, its corner close\nbutton or Escape; while it is open the page scroll is locked and focus is held inside the panel,\nreturning to the trigger when it closes. Give it a `title` and optional `subtitle`, a body, and an\noptional `footer` row of buttons. `size` is `sm` (420px), `md` (560px, the default) or `lg` (820px)."],[1366,"A named-steps progress list with a loader beside the running step, a track that fills as steps finish, and an expandable list of every step."],[828,"A number, its trend, and the agent's read on why it moved."],[907,"A one-time code as a row of single-character boxes. Typing auto-advances, Backspace steps back, the\narrows move between boxes, and pasting a full code fills them all at once. Set `status` to show the\ncode as verified or rejected."],[903,"A one-time code, one box per digit, that auto-advances and fills from a paste."],[760,"A piece of copy with the agent's edits marked inline — the old words struck, the suggestion added.\nEach edit opens a small toolbar to `accept` or `reject` it, and the header takes the rest all at\nonce. An edit the agent isn't sure of is tinted and shows a `note` above its buttons. Once every edit\nis decided, the buttons give way to a short `doneLabel`."],[310,"A pill button in the library's variants and sizes, with built-in busy, done and pressed states."],[943,"A plan the agent proposes before it starts — edit a step, skip it, add one, then approve."],[1717,"A prompt that works"],[737,"A prompt with styles, shape and count, pictures that resolve out of grain, and a gallery of earlier ones."],[1031,"A proposal card is a change the agent proposes, posted in the thread. It shows a `from` value\nchanging to the chosen option, a confidence meter that fills in a moment after the card appears\nand then follows the chosen option, and the other `options` behind an \"other options\" fold. Accept,\nNot now and Undo move it between `open`, `accepted` and `dismissed`. By default it sits flat in a\nchat thread; set `bare` to `false` to give it a card of its own."],[1172,"A queue of agent jobs in three parts — the one running with its steps and progress, the ones up next, and the ones done — with a compact toggle."],[1055,"A radar chart compares a run with its baseline on a handful of eval scores: the baseline draws itself\nas an outline, then this run grows out of the middle filled over it, on grey scale rings. Each axis\ntakes a `label`, this run's `value` and the `baseline` it is measured against, both 0 to 100; a score\nunder `weakBelow` is flagged \"Weak\". Pointing at or tabbing to an axis shows its two scores and the\nchange in the line below, and the legend shows and hides either series (one always stays)."],[1532,"A ranked list of a site's most visited pages. Each row shows its rank, title and path, a share bar\nagainst the busiest page, the view count and the percent change (green from `0`, red below). The\nrunning total rolls up, and the `Views` / `Change` sort switch slides the rows into their new order."],[1084,"A reasoning trail is the structured record the agent leaves after it thinks: a plan it works\nthrough, the notes it took, the sources it read, or the edits it made. Pick what it shows with\n`view`. The header counts the seconds and shimmers while the run is live, folds the trail away, and\nsettles on \"Reasoned for Ns\" once it finishes."],[667,"A responsive grid you drop cards or any other components into — a fixed column count or an auto-fit pack, with a gutter and per-child column spans."],[1319,"A row of KPIs, each with its change against the period before and a sparkline, with a period switch that rolls the numbers and redraws the lines."],[1157,"A run cost shows the credits a run has used against its budget, as a number and a meter. A cap\nmarks where the agent pauses, drawn as a line across the bar; when the spend reaches it the status\nturns amber and the run pauses. The breakdown under the bar shows the tokens and tool calls behind\nthe number, and the button starts, stops, resumes or raises the cap by status."],[1176,"A run queue shows a batch of agent jobs moving through three parts. The job running is raised on\nits own card with its agent, its steps, a progress bar and the time left. The jobs up next wait\nbelow, each with when it should start, and the jobs done sit under them with how long each took and\na green tick. A failed step turns its job red and offers a Retry; a Detailed / Compact switch folds\nthe running job's steps away; and once every job is done a summary takes the running job's place."],[1192,"A run summary closes out an agent run: a title glyph and a quiet subtitle, a line on what the run\ndid, and `stats` that count up from zero. Under them, `changes` list the files the run touched and\n`needs` carry what is still on the person, each with an optional action button. \"Undo all\" greys out\nthe changes and swaps the glyph; leave `undone` out to let the button toggle it, or control it."],[566,"A run that hit an error, with the steps around it and a choice of how the agent should carry on.\nEach `step` carries a `state` — `done`, `fail`, `run`, `wait`, `skip` or `hand` — that picks its\nglyph, and an optional `note` under it that crossfades when it changes. The `options` are the ways\nthe agent can continue; pick one and press Continue, and the choices fold away."],[967,"A run's progress three ways — a ring for the whole run, segments for its steps and a bar for the step in hand — with controls and an optional meter that warns as it fills."],[1211,"A scatter chart plots every `run` as a dot by its `x` (cost along the bottom) and `y` (eval score up\nthe side), sized by its `size` (tokens). Each `series` has a colour and a shape, so it reads without\ncolour too. Dashed median lines cut the plot into four named `quadrants`; pointing snaps to the\nnearest run and shows a tooltip, and a drag draws a box that selects the runs in it. Pass `ringed`\nids to circle outliers, and an `onAsk` handler to add \"Ask the agent why\" to a selection."],[1228,"A score out of 100 whose gauge is split by what goes into it, with the weakest part flagged and a fix to try."],[1252,"A search field with a leading icon and a clear button. Optional scope chips narrow the search; a\ndropdown shows recent queries while the field is empty and live results as you type, with an\noptional agent answer on top. Press Enter to search, or pick a result."],[1270,"A segmented control picks one of a few options. A white knob slides to the picked one. As `tabs`\n(the default) it switches a panel; as a `radio` group it sets an option. The arrow keys move along\nand pick, and Home and End jump to the ends. The `label` is required and names the group for screen\nreaders."],[1232,"A single score out of 100, with a half-circle gauge split into one segment per `factor`, each\nsized by its `weight` and filled by the points it `earned`. The rows under it say what each earned\nand why; the weakest factor is flagged in words, and an `improvable` row offers **Improve**, which\nasks the agent (`onImprove`) for a proposal you can **Apply** to lift that factor's points — and the\nwhole score with it."],[263,"A small label for a plan, environment, or status, with an optional dot or icon and an optional remove button."],[1337,"A status pill shows a single line of what the agent is up to. Its `state` picks the glyph and\ncolour — `thinking`, `reading` and `working` shimmer while they run — and a new `state`, `label`,\n`file` or `action` fades the old line out before the new one arrives. The `bar` variant is taller\nand draws a `progress` track along its bottom."],[1370,"A step loader shows an agent's work as named steps. The step running now sits beside a small\nloader; a track below fills as steps finish; and the row expands to the full list, each line\nswitching to its `done` text once complete. When `current` reaches the number of steps, every step\nis done and the row shows the `doneLabel`."],[1027,"A suggested change with confidence, alternatives and undo."],[327,"A surface you drop other components onto, with an optional header, body and footer, and an interactive mode that turns the whole card into a hit target."],[1420,"A thinking state is a small animated mark beside a status line. Pick one of ten marks with\n`variant`; give it `steps` and, with more than one, they take turns every `interval` unless you\ndrive the index with `current`. An elapsed-seconds counter sits at the end of the line and counts\nby itself unless you set `seconds` or hide it with `seconds={false}`."],[1433,"A thinking trace is the reasoning log an agent writes as it works. Each step arrives on a line that\nspins while it runs and turns into a green check once it finishes, with what it found written under\nit and how long it took at its right. The header counts seconds while it runs; once the last step\nlands it becomes a summary of the work and the trace folds itself away."],[1447,"A thread panel holds several conversations with an agent, one tab each, with a reply field\nunder the open one. The tabs switch which thread is shown and an underline slides to the open\none; switching staggers that thread's messages in, and a later message glides into view. Each\n`agent` message can carry `sources` — the files or pages it used — and a `thinking` message\nshimmers while the agent works on a reply."],[1484,"A toast is a short message on a surface. Its `tone` picks the icon and colour — `loading` spins —\nand it takes a `title`, an optional `description` and an optional `action` button. Pass a new `id`\nto slide a new toast in and the old one away; keep the same `id` to change the text in place."],[1467,"A toast stack collects notifications into a deck: the newest sits in front, older ones peek out\nbehind it. Hover the deck — or move the focus into it — and it fans out into a readable list; move\naway and it folds back up. Each toast takes an `icon`, a `tone` for the badge colour, a `title`, an\noptional `detail` line and a `time`. Pass the toasts oldest-first; dismiss one with its close button."],[1525,"A tool's `tip` overrides its `label` in the tooltip, so set it only when the tip should say more\nthan the button's name; the `kbd` shortcut is shown after it. A person's `working: true` swaps the\ngreen status dot for the agent's thinking dots."],[1514,"A toolbar whose labels wait a moment, then follow the pointer instantly between buttons, with a richer hover card for people."],[1518,"A tooltip is the label that waits a moment under a toolbar button, then follows the pointer\ninstantly as it moves between them. Buttons come in `groups` with a line between, each with a `tip`\nand an optional shortcut (`kbd`). Avatars at the end get a richer card instead — a person's name,\nrole and what they are doing now, with an agent shown by its thinking dots."],[1551,"A trace view lays one agent run out as a waterfall. Every span — an LLM call, a tool call, a\nretrieval, a sub-agent — sits on a shared time axis, nested under the span that started it with a\ntree's guides. Each kind has its own colour, counted in the key. While the run streams, a playhead\nmarks now and running bars glow at their leading edge; a tool call that runs over its budget turns\namber and hatched and is tagged slow. Open any span to see where it sat in the run, its input, its\noutput, its latency and its tokens."],[869,"A trigger names the picked model and how hard it thinks, over the open list of\n`models`. Each shows its `speed`, `quality` and `cost` as five-step meters, with the\nreal numbers (`speedDetail`, `qualityDetail`, `costDetail`) on hover. The thinking\neffort sits under them as a small dropdown. A model that can't take the request can\nset a `status` of `checking` or `busy` and offer a way out through `suggestion` — a\nshort notice with its own buttons, the last one filled."],[1279,"A value or a range on a stepped or continuous track, with named stops and an agent-recommended mark."],[1638,"Accessibility"],[1655,"Accessibility details specific to a component — which keys it responds to, what it announces, how it\nbehaves under reduced motion — are called out in the **Theming** and usage sections of that\ncomponent's docs page."],[39,"Account table"],[58,"Action log"],[71,"Activity heatmap"],[1664,"Add a component"],[1667,"Add a component"],[1670,"Add a widget"],[1683,"Add it from the command line:"],[1681,"Add the token stylesheet to your global styles **once** (e.g. `styles.css`, or the `styles` array\nin `angular.json`):"],[1659,"Add Visuno to a React, Angular, or Flutter project — install the package, pull the shared core once, then drop in components."],[90,"Agent inbox"],[1643,"Agent interfaces move a lot: things stream in, states change, and panels open and close. Visuno\ncomponents are built so that motion and live updates don't lock anyone out. This page covers what the\nlibrary handles and what stays your responsibility."],[112,"Agent lanes"],[117,"Agent lanes show several agents working side by side, each in its own lane and hue — its badge, its\nstep track, its `Working` tag and its stretch of the relay line that joins the badges. A working lane\nbreathes, shimmers and fills its next step; a `handoff` opens a line between two lanes and runs a\nlight down the relay line from the sender's badge to the taker's, and the stretch it crossed stays\nlit. Each `lane` takes a `name`, an `icon`, a `tone`, a `status`, the `activity` it is on, how many\n`steps` the track is cut into and how many are `done`, and an optional `log` the row opens to."],[1463,"Agent notifications that stack into a deck and fan out on hover."],[137,"Agent phases"],[142,"Agent phases show which phase the agent is in, in one line: its glyph, its name shimmering while the\nphase lasts, and the seconds so far, with a track of six short segments under it that fills phase by\nphase. When the agent needs you, is slow or fails, a line under the track says so with what to do;\nonce it is done the line reads how long it took and opens to the phases it ran through."],[152,"Agent run timeline"],[1379,"Agent tasks in a sortable table with bulk actions."],[1383,"Agent tasks in a table you can sort by any column. Pick rows with the checkboxes — or the\nselect-all in the header — and the header turns into what you can do to the selection: `pause`,\n`retry` or `cancel`. Rows glide to their new places when the order changes, cancelled rows slide\naway, and when the last task is gone the table says so."],[169,"Agent tree"],[1755,"Als Diensteanbieter sind wir gemäß § 7 Abs.1 DDG für eigene Inhalte auf diesen\nSeiten nach den allgemeinen Gesetzen verantwortlich. Nach §§ 8 bis 10 DDG sind wir\nals Diensteanbieter jedoch nicht verpflichtet, übermittelte oder gespeicherte fremde\nInformationen zu überwachen oder nach Umständen zu forschen, die auf eine\nrechtswidrige Tätigkeit hinweisen."],[63,"An action log is a streaming list of what the agent does: each read, search, edit or command\nlands as a row, running with a spinner, then done with a check and the time it took. A row with\noutput opens to show it, like a diff or test run. The header shows a spinner while the run is live\nand a green dot once it finishes."],[76,"An activity heatmap shows when something is busy, as a 7×24 grid of cells — one a day down, one\nan hour across — each tinted from the accent by its value. Pass one or more `series`; each is 168\nvalues from 0 to 1 (Monday 12am first), with a `max` and `unit` for the tips and an optional\n`note`. With two or more series a switch appears, and picking one sweeps it in as a wave from the\ntop left. In a narrow card the grid shows half the day at a time in bigger cells and scrolls\nsideways, starting at the latest hours."],[157,"An agent run timeline draws one run as a Gantt chart. Every step is a bar on a shared time axis:\nthe model thinking or writing (purple), a tool like a file read or a test run (teal), or an app the\nagent works in, such as Figma or GitHub (orange), each with its own mark. A playhead marks now while\nthe run streams and the lanes it has passed take a faint wash; the step at work breathes a ring round\nits icon and its bar carries a bright leading edge. A failed step hatches red and tags its label, and\na retry hangs a dashed line off the end of the step it retries. The key under the title counts how\nlong each kind has run, and the footer adds up the total time, cost, steps and retries. Once the run\nis done the playhead stops at the finish and turns green."],[174,"An agent tree shows an orchestrator and its sub-agents as a tree. Each row has its `name`, its\n`task`, a status glyph and a `cost`; a branch's cost is its own plus everything under it, and the\nheader rolls up the whole run. A chevron folds a branch, and the arrow keys move through it as in\nany tree view. A failed step can carry a `fix` — a button under its task that offers the way out."],[1103,"An answer with its earlier versions a click away. The arrows page through every `versions` entry\n(oldest first); a different version fades the old text out, then the new one arrives word by word.\nThe regenerate button calls `onRegenerate` so you can add a version and show it, and the copy button\ncopies the version on screen. While `streaming` is set the new words fade in as they arrive, paging\nwaits, and the `status` beside the buttons shimmers."],[1300,"An answer with its sources folded into one quiet pill. Open the pill to see each source as a row —\nits favicon, where it came from and a small bar for how much it counted — and use the filter chips\nto pick by `kind` (`web`, `file`, `figma`, …). Each source takes a `title`, a `host`, a `relevance`\nfrom 0 to 1, a `mark` for the favicon square and its `color`; give one an `onOpen` and it grows an\nopen button on hover. Leave `open` and `filter` out to let the pill and chips drive themselves."],[371,"An answer with numbered citations. Hovering, focusing or tapping one shows its source in a small card\nunderneath — the coloured mark and site, the title, the passage the answer relies on (with the part\nthat matters highlighted) and a link. The card rises in place; moving to another citation replays the\nentrance. It takes the answer `text` (where `[n]` cites source n and a blank line starts a new\nparagraph) and the list of `sources`."],[545,"An email the agent drafted, laid out like the email it is: who it goes to, the `subject`, the `body`\nand any `attachments`. Check and edit it, then send now or later — the menu beside the button picks\n`when` — or `discard` it. A `warning` can stop it before it sends, like an attachment the message\nmentions but nothing attached, and after sending there is a short window to `undo`. The `phase` drives\nwhat the footer shows."],[541,"An email the agent drafted, to check and edit before it sends."],[583,"An eval run case by case, against the run before."],[587,"An eval suite's latest run: one bar segment per case, the pass count against the run before, and\nthe cases — all of them, or only the failed ones. The `Run evals` button spins while the suite runs\nand the count reads `Running 3 of 8`. A failed case opens to show what was `expected` and what it\n`got`, with a `fix` button that reruns just that case."],[735,"An icon has no colour of its own: it strokes with `currentColor`, so it matches the text it sits in\nand switches with light and dark (`data-theme=\"dark\"`) automatically. Set a colour by styling the\nelement (web) or the `color` parameter (Flutter). The 24×24 grid, 1.75 stroke width and round caps\nare shared across all four frameworks, so the same glyph looks identical in each."],[170,"An orchestrator and its sub-agents, with cost rolled up per branch."],[1740,"Angaben gemäß § 5 DDG"],[192,"Answer action bar"],[810,"Answers that stream in with numbered sources and next steps."],[1645,"Anything you can click, you can reach and operate from the keyboard. Focus order follows reading\norder, overlays (modals, popovers) trap focus while open and return it to the trigger on close, and\nEscape dismisses transient surfaces."],[1669,"Apply the theme"],[216,"Artifact card"],[244,"Attachment tray"],[262,"Badge"],[274,"Bar chart"],[1735,"Because the components are designed for agent interfaces, they map cleanly onto the states an AI\nproduct moves through — thinking, reading, working, waiting for review, streaming an answer, logging\nactions. Browse the **Agent status** and **Conversation** groups in the sidebar for the building\nblocks, and compose them into your own flows."],[291,"Browser session"],[1718,"Building agents with Visuno"],[309,"Buttons"],[326,"Card"],[1705,"Change a token once and it flows to every component under it."],[1709,"Change a token once and the change is everywhere — that is the whole point of the system."],[342,"Change review"],[348,"Change review walks a set of changed `files` as a diff. Pick a file in the list, then **keep** or\n**undo** each hunk on its own — Keep turns to a green done chip, an undone hunk's Undo sits on the\nsurface, and pressing either again takes the choice back. **Keep all** settles the current file and\nsteps to the next; **Undo all** settles it in place. Hover a line to reveal its comment button and\nask the agent about that change. The file list rolls each file up to kept, undone or part-decided."],[1347,"Change the agent's course while it works, without starting over."],[1,"Changelog"],[366,"Citation hover card"],[1120,"Click a sentence in the document and a small toolbar offers rewrites — `Clearer`, `Shorter`,\n`Friendlier` by default. Pick one and `onRewrite` is called; while it works the toolbar says\nRewriting, then the answer is written in beside the old sentence word by word, struck-through\nold against the new. Keep it with Accept or put it back with Undo. The toolbar sits above the\nsentence, or under it when above would cover the header."],[387,"Code the agent wrote, with line notes you can ask about."],[386,"Code viewer"],[1615,"Common questions about Visuno — frameworks, theming, licensing, and how the components ship."],[35,"Component documentation updated and **sections reorganised** for clarity (Agent status,\nConversation, Charts and tables, Dates, Forms and inputs)."],[1680,"Components are **standalone** — import the one you need directly into your component's `imports`, no\nmodule boilerplate."],[1636,"Components are built with semantic markup, keyboard support, and reduced-motion handling\nwhere animation is involved. Accessibility notes specific to a component are called out on\nits docs page."],[1775,"Components are grouped by what they do in an agent product:"],[1647,"Components that animate — streaming status, shimmering states, fades between lines — honour\n`prefers-reduced-motion`. When a viewer asks their OS for less motion, animations are shortened or\nremoved and content simply appears, with no loss of information."],[1648,"Components that update on their own (status pills, toasts, live answers) announce changes through\nARIA live regions so screen-reader users hear what changed without having to go looking for it."],[1644,"Components use the right element for the job — real `<button>`s for actions, headings and lists where\nthey belong, and ARIA roles only where native semantics fall short. Interactive controls expose a\nname, role, and state to assistive tech."],[407,"Composer"],[429,"Context meter"],[193,"Copy, regenerate, rate and listen to an answer from one row under it."],[40,"Customer accounts with health, plan and an agent note per row."],[44,"Customer accounts you can search and filter by plan. Each row shows the logo, plan tag, seats, a\nhealth bar and the MRR; click a row and it opens to the note the agent left — with an optional\naction button (like **Draft email**) that spins until its promise settles, then shows its done\nlabel. The rows stagger in again whenever the search or the filter changes."],[1692,"Dark mode"],[447,"Decision prompt"],[466,"Design review"],[1774,"Design tokens (`--vo-*` on the web, `VoColors`/`VoSpace`/`VoRadius` in Flutter) drive every color,\nspace, radius, and motion — change a token once and it flows everywhere."],[486,"Design tuner"],[1803,"Design-tool assets (Figma kit) matched to the shipped components."],[504,"Details panel"],[1758,"Die durch die Seitenbetreiber erstellten Inhalte und Werke auf diesen Seiten\nunterliegen dem deutschen Urheberrecht. Die Vervielfältigung, Bearbeitung,\nVerbreitung und jede Art der Verwertung außerhalb der Grenzen des Urheberrechtes\nbedürfen der schriftlichen Zustimmung des jeweiligen Autors bzw. Erstellers."],[1753,"Die Europäische Kommission stellt eine Plattform zur Online-Streitbeilegung (OS)\nbereit: https://ec.europa.eu/consumers/odr"],[34,"Docs landing page **redesigned** — a quieter hero with live, self-animating component\npreviews and the full library advertised as preview cards."],[525,"Donut chart"],[540,"Draft review"],[1283,"Drag a thumb to set a value, or two thumbs for a `[low, high]` range. Give it discrete `step`s and\na labelled scale with `stops`, and mark a suggested value with `recommended`. Keyboard works too —\narrows step, Page keys jump by ten, Home/End go to the ends."],[727,"Draw an icon by its `name`, or pass your own `path` / SVG for a glyph the set does not have. Icons\nuse `currentColor`, so they take the colour of the surrounding text; `size` ranges from `xs` to `xl`."],[245,"Drop files for the agent and watch each one upload."],[1750,"E-Mail: info@sdkindustries.de"],[289,"Each `BarChartBar` carries `values: number[]` — one per series, stacked from the bottom — so the\nseries order sets the order of the parts, the legend and the breakdown in the readout."],[1077,"Each `RadialGoal` carries a `key`, a `label` (like \"Support agent\"), a `unit` (what it counts, like\n\"tickets\"), the `value` done, its `target`, and an optional `color`. The share is `value / target`;\nat or past the target the ring's row shows an \"on target\" chip, and the ring draws a second lap with\na marker. Colours fall back to the chart palette (the accent, then green, orange, violet and red)."],[1330,"Each `StatCardsStat` carries `values: { value, change, points }[]` — one entry per period, in the\nsame order as `periods` — where `value` is the number, `change` is the percent against the period\nbefore, and `points` is the sparkline oldest first."],[1066,"Each agent's share of its own target as a ring, with the overall score in the middle."],[1766,"Each component in these docs has a **live preview** you can toggle between light/dark and the two\nstyles, plus the code for all three frameworks side by side."],[1494,"Each tool the agent calls, with its input and output."],[561,"Error recovery"],[582,"Eval results"],[197,"Every action on an answer in one raised rail under it: **copy** it, **regenerate** it — which\nkeeps each version and pages between them — give it a **thumbs** up or down (a down asks what was\noff, and the reason can write a new version that answers it), and **read it aloud**, which widens\nthe button into a progress bar and a clock and lights each word as it is read. Pass `versions`\noldest first; each version carries its own `meta`, its own `rating`, and an optional `changed`\nphrase that is highlighted as what moved from the version before."],[1771,"Every component responds to the same two switches, so your whole UI changes at once:"],[1720,"Every docs page is available as plain text and Markdown, and the whole site is summarised in two\n`llms.txt` files that follow the llms.txt convention."],[1646,"Every interactive element has a visible focus ring drawn from the `--vo-focus` token, so keyboard\nusers can always see where they are. The ring respects the active theme and style."],[153,"Every model and tool step of a run on one time axis — a Gantt chart with a playhead, the time each step takes, and the cost as it adds up."],[1207,"Every run as a dot by cost and eval score, sized by tokens, with quadrants and a drag to select."],[91,"Every run that needs you, is running or is done, in one list."],[59,"Every tool call the agent makes, as a list that grows while it works, where each row opens to show its output."],[1697,"Every Visuno component reads from one shared set of **design tokens**, so you theme the whole UI from\none place instead of styling components one by one. There are two switches everyone uses out of the\nbox — **dark mode** and **style** — and underneath them, the tokens you can override to make the\nlibrary your own."],[1296,"Everything the agent read, folded into one quiet pill."],[1788,"Exploring"],[1614,"FAQ"],[602,"Feedback sheet"],[623,"Field extraction"],[1617,"Frameworks"],[646,"Funnel chart"],[1616,"General"],[1751,"Geschäftsführer: Simon Kobler"],[1764,"Get started"],[1715,"Give an assistant the context"],[666,"Grid"],[1745,"Haftung für Inhalte"],[1746,"Haftung für Links"],[679,"Health score"],[367,"Hover a citation to see the exact passage it came from."],[430,"How full the context is and what fills it, with a Compact action to free room."],[1696,"How it fits together"],[1639,"How Visuno components handle semantics, keyboard, focus, contrast, and reduced motion — and what's still on you."],[699,"Human handoff"],[722,"Icon"],[1801,"Ideas we're considering but haven't committed to. Feedback shapes what graduates to\n_Planned_:"],[736,"Image generation"],[741,"Image generation is a prompt with style chips, a shape and how many pictures, and an optional\nmodel; the pictures it makes resolve the way an image model works — Composing (a grid of dots in\nthe picture's colours grows out of grain), Adding detail (the blur lifts while a scan line sweeps\ndown and leaves the picture behind it) and Upscaling (the last softness and grain go). Every\npicture moves at its own speed, one can fail with Retry, and a finished one can be upscaled,\nvaried, downloaded, opened larger or used as a reference. Beside it sit a gallery of earlier\npictures, newest first, and a few style presets; narrower, they take the feed's place under a\nCreate / Gallery / Styles switch. `onGenerate` makes the pictures and reports how far each has come."],[1679,"Import the component and drop it in:"],[1678,"Import the shared tokens and base styles **once**, near the root of your app (e.g. in `layout.tsx`\nor your global CSS entry):"],[1738,"Imprint"],[340,"In Angular the header end slot and the footer are projected with the `card-action` and `card-footer`\nattributes; in Flutter `action` is a widget and `footer` a list of widgets."],[1786,"In progress"],[677,"In React and Flutter a spanning child is wrapped in `GridItem` / `VoGridItem`; in Angular it carries\nthe `gridItem` directive."],[38,"Initial commit — docs site scaffolded on Next.js and Fumadocs."],[755,"Inline text diff"],[1662,"Install the package"],[1665,"Install the package"],[1668,"Install the package"],[1688,"Install the package and pull the core once, and every component you add after that just works — it\nreads the same tokens and inherits your theme automatically."],[1658,"Installation"],[1759,"Introduction"],[1741,"Kontakt"],[772,"Launch checklist"],[1739,"Legal information for the Visuno documentation site, operated by SDK Industries (Simon Kobler IT- und Mediendienstleistungen)."],[36,"Light wordmark asset added so the logo reads correctly in dark mode."],[788,"Line chart"],[809,"Live answer"],[1153,"Live credit and token spend against a budget, with a cap the agent pauses at."],[1714,"Machine-readable docs"],[408,"Message input with attachments, what to work on, a mode and dictation."],[827,"Metric insight"],[848,"Modal"],[864,"Model picker"],[1528,"Most visited pages with share of traffic and a quick sort."],[1760,"Native UI components for AI-agent products, in three frameworks."],[1671,"Next steps"],[6,"Notable changes to the Visuno component library and these docs, newest first.\nComponent source lives in the components repository."],[1804,"Nothing here is a promise of a date. The changelog records what has\nactually shipped; this page records intent."],[885,"Number field"],[1698,"On the web (React and Angular) both switches are attributes on a parent element; on Flutter they live\nin the theme object. Every component below the switch responds at once."],[1547,"One agent run as an observability waterfall — every span on a shared time axis, nested under the span that started it, with its tokens and latency."],[1266,"One choice from a few views or options, with a white knob that slides to the picked one. Works as tabs or as a radio group, fully keyboard driven."],[138,"One line for which phase an agent is in, from thinking to done, that stops to ask, says when it is slow, and folds to how long it took."],[1333,"One live line for what an agent is doing right now, fading between its states and resizing to fit them."],[684,"One score for a design system, with the checks behind it. A half-gauge shows the overall score\n(green from 90, the accent from 80, red below). Point at a check — or tab to it — and the gauge\nswings to that check's score and names it; leave the list and it swings back. Open a check to see\nwhy, and, when it is `fixable` and you pass `onFix`, fix it from there: the button spins until your\npromise settles, then you update the check's `score` and `detail`."],[1480,"One toast at a time — a new one rises in while the last one slips away."],[734,"Only the ~113 names the library itself uses are compiled into each bundle; every other name resolves\non demand on the web. On Angular and Flutter, names outside the compiled set are drawn by passing\n`path` or `svg`, so any of the ~5,200 icons — and your own — render everywhere."],[1637,"Open an issue on the issue tracker. Planned\nwork and what's shipped are tracked on the roadmap and\nchangelog."],[1684,"Or add it to your `pubspec.yaml` and run `flutter pub get`:"],[902,"OTP field"],[1695,"Override a token"],[1140,"Pass your model as `ai` to replace the demo writer — return a string, a promise, or an async\niterable of chunks to stream the suggestion in as the model answers."],[1642,"Per-component notes"],[916,"Permission gate"],[1635,"Pick a component from the sidebar, copy the snippet for your framework, and drop it in.\nPull the shared core once (tokens + helpers), and every component you add after that just\nworks."],[1782,"Pick a component from the sidebar, copy the snippet for your framework, and drop it in. Pull the\nshared core once (tokens + helpers), and every component you add just works."],[865,"Pick a model by speed, quality and cost, and how hard it thinks."],[1725,"Pick whichever fits your tool:"],[1676,"Pick your framework. Each starts by installing the package, then pulling the shared core once."],[942,"Plan review"],[1787,"Planned"],[1713,"Point your AI coding assistant at Visuno — llms.txt, per-page Markdown, and prompts that get correct, on-token output."],[1660,"Prerequisites"],[966,"Progress"],[971,"Progress shows a long run three ways at once: a ring for the whole run, a segment per step, and a\nbar for how far the step in hand has got. The `state` colours it — grey while indeterminate or\npaused, the accent while running, red with a Retry on error, green when complete — and the controls\nfollow (Pause, Resume, Run again). An optional `meter` underneath warns in words as it fills."],[983,"Prompt library"],[1006,"Prompt starters"],[1011,"Prompt starters are suggested first prompts shown as cards, two to a row. Picking one writes its\nfull `prompt` into the field word by word and marks the card as picked; pressing Send emits the\nprompt and clears the field with a sent line. Pass `onShuffle` to add a Shuffle button and swap in\nanother set of `starters` — a new set staggers the cards in again."],[1716,"Prompting tips"],[42,"Properties"],[61,"Properties"],[74,"Properties"],[93,"Properties"],[115,"Properties"],[140,"Properties"],[155,"Properties"],[172,"Properties"],[195,"Properties"],[219,"Properties"],[247,"Properties"],[265,"Properties"],[277,"Properties"],[294,"Properties"],[312,"Properties"],[329,"Properties"],[345,"Properties"],[369,"Properties"],[389,"Properties"],[410,"Properties"],[432,"Properties"],[450,"Properties"],[469,"Properties"],[489,"Properties"],[507,"Properties"],[528,"Properties"],[543,"Properties"],[564,"Properties"],[585,"Properties"],[605,"Properties"],[626,"Properties"],[649,"Properties"],[669,"Properties"],[682,"Properties"],[702,"Properties"],[725,"Properties"],[739,"Properties"],[758,"Properties"],[775,"Properties"],[791,"Properties"],[812,"Properties"],[830,"Properties"],[851,"Properties"],[867,"Properties"],[888,"Properties"],[905,"Properties"],[919,"Properties"],[945,"Properties"],[969,"Properties"],[986,"Properties"],[1009,"Properties"],[1029,"Properties"],[1053,"Properties"],[1068,"Properties"],[1082,"Properties"],[1101,"Properties"],[1118,"Properties"],[1137,"Properties"],[1155,"Properties"],[1174,"Properties"],[1190,"Properties"],[1209,"Properties"],[1230,"Properties"],[1250,"Properties"],[1268,"Properties"],[1281,"Properties"],[1298,"Properties"],[1321,"Properties"],[1335,"Properties"],[1349,"Properties"],[1368,"Properties"],[1381,"Properties"],[1398,"Properties"],[1418,"Properties"],[1431,"Properties"],[1445,"Properties"],[1465,"Properties"],[1482,"Properties"],[1496,"Properties"],[1516,"Properties"],[1530,"Properties"],[1549,"Properties"],[1565,"Properties"],[1584,"Properties"],[1603,"Properties"],[505,"Properties of a run you can change in place."],[1026,"Proposal card"],[1663,"Pull the core"],[1666,"Pull the core"],[1050,"Radar chart"],[1065,"Radial goals"],[1070,"Radial goals are concentric rings, one per agent, each its share of its own target. The overall\nscore sits in the middle. Past the target a ring goes round again from the top, with a marker where\nit ends, so an agent that is ahead reads as a second lap. Hovering or focusing a ring — or its\nlegend row — dims the rest and rolls the middle to that agent's share. A `ranges` switch (like Week\nand Month) repaints every ring."],[603,"Rate an answer and tell the agent what missed."],[1630,"React, Angular, and Flutter use their usual toolchains."],[1079,"Reasoning trail"],[1099,"Regenerate an answer and flip between versions."],[1098,"Response versions"],[1115,"Rewrite toolbar"],[1134,"Rich text editor"],[1783,"Roadmap"],[1152,"Run cost"],[1171,"Run queue"],[1187,"Run summary"],[984,"Saved prompts with blanks to fill in before they go to the agent."],[1206,"Scatter chart"],[1227,"Score breakdown"],[1247,"Search input"],[1248,"Search with scoped filters, recent queries and an inline agent answer."],[1763,"Sections"],[1265,"Segmented Control"],[1116,"Select a sentence and let the agent rewrite it in place."],[1632,"Set `data-theme=\"dark\"` on a parent element (web), or use `Brightness.dark` in the Flutter\ntheme. Every component reads the same tokens, so your whole UI flips at once."],[1699,"Set dark mode once, high up, and the whole subtree flips."],[1661,"Set up"],[113,"Several agents working in parallel, each with its own lane and hand-offs."],[1396,"Shell commands the agent runs, with their output as it streams in."],[1785,"Shipped"],[1620,"Short answers to the questions that come up most. If something isn't covered here,\nopen an issue on GitHub."],[647,"Signup to paid, with the biggest drop called out by the agent."],[1749,"Simon Kobler IT- und Mediendienstleistungen\nBürgermeister-Wild-Straße 33a\n85521 Ottobrunn\nDeutschland"],[1278,"Slider"],[471,"Something the agent made, laid out for sign-off. One column runs the `checks`; the other holds the\n`messages` — a teammate's verdict, the agent's reply — oldest first. The `phase` carries it from\n`review` to `approved` (the Ship button turns on), then `shipping`, and finally `live` or\n`scheduled`. Beside the Ship button a menu picks how it ships from your `options`."],[1295,"Source stack"],[1567,"Speech to text that shows what it heard as it hears it, and lets the person fix the words it was\nunsure of. Each word fades in as it arrives and the wave scrolls while it records. Press Done to\nmove from recording into review, where unsure words are highlighted — tap one to pick from a short\n\"Did you mean\" menu — then Done again to send, or Cancel to drop it. Once recording stops the box\nkeeps its height: the wave freezes as the whole recording and the words stay where they were."],[1318,"Stat cards"],[1323,"Stat cards are a row of numbers that matter. Each card has an icon, a label, the value in large\ntype, its change against the period before (green from zero, red below), and a sparkline in its own\ncolour. A `period` switch along the top rolls the numbers to their new values and redraws every\nline."],[1332,"Status pill"],[1346,"Steering"],[1351,"Steering shows a run you can nudge while it works: its `steps`, the log of what you told it and\nwhat it said back, and a message box. A message waits for the step in progress, then the agent\ntakes it up from the next one — so you change its course without starting over. When your steer\ntouches work already done, the agent raises an amber heads-up with `actions` (like *Leave it* or\n*Redo the Pro card*) and settles on an `answer` once you choose."],[1365,"Step Loader"],[1744,"Streitschlichtung"],[1693,"Style"],[1007,"Suggested tasks that fill the composer in one click."],[1443,"Switch between agent threads and reply in place."],[1563,"Talk to the agent, then fix what it misheard before sending."],[1378,"Task table"],[1416,"Ten animated indicators, each a small mark beside a status line that steps through what an agent is doing, with an optional elapsed-seconds counter."],[1395,"Terminal stream"],[1629,"That's the goal — one design, three frameworks. React leads, and the Angular and Flutter\nports follow. Each component's docs page shows the code for every framework it currently\nships in, side by side."],[452,"The agent asks a few quick questions before it starts — one at a time, with a row of\noptions for each. Number keys pick, **Enter** continues and **Skip** lets the agent decide.\nOnce every question is answered the body settles into a summary and a line that says the\nagent is getting going; pass `started` to settle that line with a check."],[448,"The agent asks a few quick questions before it starts."],[921,"The agent asks before it acts: one card with the question, the `scopes` to tick, and a footer\nwith how long the grant lasts (`durations`) and the two answers. Once allowed it gives way to a\nshorter result row — a spinner while the agent is `working`, a green check when done — with a\nRevoke button; declining shows an undoable notice. A follow-up ask mid-run (`followUp`) turns the\nicon tile amber."],[917,"The agent asks before it touches something new."],[292,"The agent browsing the web for you step by step, in a small browser frame with the steps it takes, and Pause and Take over."],[773,"The agent checks a design before it ships, and fixes what fails."],[700,"The agent hands a conversation to a person, with what they need to pick it up."],[95,"The agent inbox puts every run in one list, grouped by what it needs: **Needs you**, **Running** and\n**Done**. A running run shows a live clock; one that waits for you offers **Approve**, and one that\nfailed turns red and offers **Retry**. When a run changes group it glides to its new place, an empty\ngroup goes away, and the line beside the title says what is left."],[704,"The agent passes a conversation to a person: why it handed off (`reason`), who has it (`assignee`,\nwith a `people` list for **Reassign**), when the reply is due (`secondsLeft` counts down and the bar\nempties, turning orange under five minutes), the `summary` and `facts` they need to pick it up, and\nthe `message` the customer was told (editable in place when you pass `onMessageChange`). A `status`\nrow at the foot says where it stands — neutral while it waits, amber when the person can't take it,\ngreen once they have."],[624,"The agent pulls fields out of a document and shows where each came from."],[628,"The agent reads a document on the left and lists the fields it found on the right, each with\nhow sure it is. A new field slides in and its source region on the page lights up for a moment,\nso you can see where each value came from. A field the agent is unsure of (`confidence` under\n`0.6`) asks the person to check it — they type the value into the field itself and confirm with\nEnter or by clicking away. The Send button waits until every field is sure or checked."],[756,"The agent's edits to a piece of copy, accepted or rejected one by one."],[1080,"The agent's plan, notes, sources or edits, under a header it can fold away."],[1601,"The agent's web searches as a trail — each query and what came back, each page read, skimmed or skipped and why, and an answer whose citations light their pages."],[391,"The code the agent wrote, across one or more files. The lines it changed are marked — their\nnumber turns green and the line gets a wash — and the `+N changed` count in the header follows the\nopen file. Click a line and the agent explains it: a note folds open under the line, reads for a\nmoment, then crossfades to what it says. The copy button copies the open file's text."],[412,"The composer is the box you write to the agent in — not a plain field but a small component on a\nsurface. Text grows as you type; attachments load as chips above it and hold Send until they are\nready (a failed one shows in red with a Try again button). Two menus set what the agent works on\n(`contexts`) and what it may do (`modes`), a microphone swaps the toolbar for a dictation bar, and\nSend fires `onSubmit` and clears the box. `value`, `attachments`, `context` and `mode` are\ncontrollable — leave the matching input out to let the composer keep that piece itself."],[1633,"The default **Blueprint** style is crisp and squared; **Soft** is rounded and plush. Switch\nto Soft on the web with `data-style=\"soft\"` on a parent element."],[1649,"The default light and dark themes are tuned to meet WCAG AA contrast for text and essential UI.\nMeaning is never carried by color alone — states also use a glyph, label, or shape."],[1264,"The field, scope chips and dropdown all draw from the shared `--vo-*` tokens, so they work in light\nand dark (`data-theme=\"dark\"`) and in the Soft style (`data-style=\"soft\"`). The field shows focus\nwith its own edge (no extra ring); the agent answer sits on a faint accent tint."],[1151,"The Flutter port approximates the contenteditable surface (which the web uses natively): the body is a\nread-oriented rich view driven through a `RichTextEditorController`, with the same toolbar, word count\nand streamed Ask-AI flow. See the file header for the full list of approximations."],[1682,"The Flutter widgets are themed to match the web. Wrap your app in the Visuno theme once, then use\nthe widgets anywhere."],[678,"The grid is pure layout — it adds no color, shadow or border of its own, so the children it holds\n(cards, charts, tables) carry the look. Spacing flows through the shared `--vo-s-*` tokens. It works\nunchanged in light and dark and in both the Soft and Blueprint styles."],[405,"The highlight tokens are `k` keyword, `s` string, `f` function, `cm` comment, `tg` tag and `at`\nattribute."],[1650,"The library gives you accessible building blocks; the composition is yours. A few things to get\nright in your app:"],[1729,"The library has a few hard rules; stating them up front keeps generated code correct and on-brand:"],[1429,"The live trace an agent writes as it works, each step turning green as it finishes, then folded away into a summary."],[1421,"The marks are `orbit` (a light running round a ring, the line shimmering), `morph` (a rectangle,\ncircle and triangle in turn), `scan` (a column sweeping rows of dots), `stream` (dots filling in,\nthe line typed out behind a caret), `matrix` (a dot grid twinkling at random), `stack` (pages\nshuffling to the front), `pulse` (a core breathing with sonar rings growing out of it), `wave`\n(bars rising and falling in a travelling waveform), `bounce` (three dots hopping in turn) and\n`spiral` (dots lighting up along an inward spiral toward the centre)."],[947,"The plan an agent posts before it starts work: each step is a line you can rewrite, skip (it stays,\nstruck through) or add to. The footer's **Approve plan** hands it off; from there the numbers turn\ninto a spinner, a check, or a retry clock, and a step can open a quiet line under it reporting what it\nfound or what it waits on. In `review` the steps are editable; in `running` the agent works through\nthem; in `done` the footer offers **See changes**."],[1623,"The React build is the reference implementation and is stable. Angular and Flutter are\nnative ports that mirror the same props and behaviour. As with any pre-1.0 library, pin a\nversion and read the changelog before upgrading."],[1677,"The React components are plain React 18+ components with no framework lock-in, so they work in\nNext.js, Vite, Remix, or any React setup."],[1770,"The React version is the reference; Angular and Flutter are native ports that mirror the same props\nand behavior."],[1628,"The React version is the reference; Angular and Flutter mirror the same props and behaviour."],[988,"The studio's saved prompts, each with blanks written as `{key}`. Opening one lays its blanks out as\nfields — typed, or picked from a short list — and the prompt fills in as they change: filled blanks\ntake the accent colour, empty ones read by name. A prompt can't be used until every blank has a\nvalue; try it empty and each missing blank turns red and says what it needs."],[1694,"The token system"],[1498,"The tools an agent calls, shown between its message and its answer. Each call takes a `server`,\na `name` and a `state` — `queued` waits, `running` spins, `done` shows how long it took and what\ncame back. Click a call to fold its `input` and `output` open or shut. The `reply` fades in once\nevery call is done."],[1691,"The two switches"],[43,"Theming"],[62,"Theming"],[75,"Theming"],[94,"Theming"],[116,"Theming"],[141,"Theming"],[156,"Theming"],[173,"Theming"],[196,"Theming"],[220,"Theming"],[248,"Theming"],[266,"Theming"],[278,"Theming"],[295,"Theming"],[313,"Theming"],[330,"Theming"],[347,"Theming"],[370,"Theming"],[390,"Theming"],[411,"Theming"],[433,"Theming"],[451,"Theming"],[470,"Theming"],[490,"Theming"],[508,"Theming"],[529,"Theming"],[544,"Theming"],[565,"Theming"],[586,"Theming"],[606,"Theming"],[627,"Theming"],[650,"Theming"],[670,"Theming"],[683,"Theming"],[703,"Theming"],[726,"Theming"],[740,"Theming"],[759,"Theming"],[776,"Theming"],[792,"Theming"],[813,"Theming"],[831,"Theming"],[852,"Theming"],[868,"Theming"],[889,"Theming"],[906,"Theming"],[920,"Theming"],[946,"Theming"],[970,"Theming"],[987,"Theming"],[1010,"Theming"],[1030,"Theming"],[1054,"Theming"],[1069,"Theming"],[1083,"Theming"],[11